---
import CodeBlock from "../../components/ui/CodeBlock.astro";
import ModelSection from "../../components/models/ModelSection.astro";
import ModelLayout from "../../layouts/ModelLayout.astro";
import { sectionLookup } from "../../lib/models";

const sections = [
  {
    id: "how-it-works",
    title: "How it works",
  },
  {
    id: "model-at-a-glance",
    title: "Model at a glance",
  },
  {
    id: "example-input-and-output",
    title: "Example input and output",
  },
  {
    id: "examples-for-claims-handlers-and-loss-adjusters",
    title: "Examples for claims handlers and loss adjusters",
  },
  {
    id: "quickstart",
    title: "Quickstart",
  },
  {
    id: "provenance",
    title: "Base model and data",
  },
  {
    id: "limitations",
    title: "Limitations",
  },
  {
    id: "license-and-citation",
    title: "License and citation",
  },
];
const section = sectionLookup(sections);
---

<ModelLayout slug="nouli-insurance-loss-event" sections={sections}>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Classify an initial property-claim notice across 22 loss-event categories, then suggest an
      intake queue. Explore recorded FNOL examples for claims handlers, intake teams and loss
      adjusters.
    </p>
    <p class="release-scope">
      Research preview, evaluated on synthetic English household and building insurance notices.
      Coverage and settlement decisions are outside its scope.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
  <ModelSection {...section("how-it-works")}>
    <p>
      <img
        src="/models/nouli-insurance-loss-event/how-it-works.svg"
        alt="An initial claim narrative goes to the loss-event classifier, which returns a category and probabilities. A deterministic Nouli routing rule then selects an intake queue or review."
      />
    </p>
    <p>
      The model identifies the loss event from the narrative. A separate, hand-authored rule maps
      its answer to a queue. The included Nouli workflow connects the two steps and records their
      execution.
    </p>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("model-at-a-glance")}>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Property</th>
          <th>Value</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Input</td>
          <td>Initial property-claim narrative in English</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Output</td>
          <td>One of 22 loss events, probabilities and an illustrative queue</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Categories</td>
          <td>Water damage, fire, theft, weather events and other property-loss causes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Runtime</td>
          <td>Local CPU or CUDA; CPU rechecked for this release</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Base model</td>
          <td>RoBERTa-large</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>License</td>
          <td>Apache-2.0; upstream MIT notice applies</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("example-input-and-output")}>
    <p>
      <strong>Input:</strong> “Sewage backed up through the bathroom drain and damaged the floor.”
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Recorded output</strong> (probabilities and trace fields omitted):
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Recorded output"
      code={`{
  "answers": {
    "loss_event": {"choice": "Backflow waste"}
  },
  "routing": {
    "queue": "water_damage"
  }
}
`}
    />
    <p>
      The specialist identifies sewage backflow, then the routing rule assigns the water-damage
      queue. This example was run with the supplied weights on CPU.{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/nouli-ai/nouli-insurance-loss-event/resolve/57e6cf45e097d6280a224ba18cd11f6120eeb28e/examples/notice.txt">
        Input
      </a>{" "}
      ·{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/nouli-ai/nouli-insurance-loss-event/resolve/57e6cf45e097d6280a224ba18cd11f6120eeb28e/examples/output.json">
        Complete output
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("examples-for-claims-handlers-and-loss-adjusters")}>
    <p>
      These constructed first-notice-of-loss (FNOL) examples cover all 22 model categories. Each
      result was recorded on CPU.{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/nouli-ai/nouli-insurance-loss-event/resolve/57e6cf45e097d6280a224ba18cd11f6120eeb28e/examples/category-examples.json">
        Complete inputs, probabilities and queue outputs
      </a>
      .
    </p>
    <p>
      For an intake handoff, retain the original notice alongside the suggested event and queue. The
      reported cause matters: a broken window can follow hail, forced entry or an accidental impact.
      Policy coverage and settlement require a separate assessment.
    </p>
    <details class="model-example-group" open>
      <summary>
        Water-related notices<span>5 examples</span>
      </summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Example queue: <code>water_damage</code>.
        </p>
        <table class="example-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Reported FNOL</th>
              <th>Recorded loss event</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>
                During prolonged heavy rain, water entered through a poorly sealed roof joint and
                stained the bedroom ceiling.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Atmospheric precipitation</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Sewage backed up through the bathroom drain and damaged the floor.</td>
              <td>
                <strong>Backflow waste</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                The nearby river overflowed its banks and floodwater entered the ground floor of our
                home, damaging flooring and furniture.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Flood</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                Groundwater rose beneath the house after weeks of wet weather and seeped through the
                basement walls and floor.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Groundwater</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                A pipe beneath the kitchen sink burst, releasing water across the floor and damaging
                the lower cabinets.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Waterleakage</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="model-example-group">
      <summary>
        Theft, forced entry and malicious damage<span>4 examples</span>
      </summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Example queue: <code>crime_damage</code>.
        </p>
        <table class="example-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Reported FNOL</th>
              <th>Recorded loss event</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>
                Our home was broken into while we were on holiday. The intruders smashed a rear
                window to enter and took jewellery from an upstairs bedroom.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Burglary</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                A person threatened me with a knife outside my front door and forced me to hand over
                my handbag and phone.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Robbery</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                My bicycle was taken from the front garden while I was inside. No doors, locks or
                windows were damaged.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Theft</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                Someone deliberately sprayed paint across our outside wall and smashed the garden
                lights. Nothing was taken.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Vandalism</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="model-example-group">
      <summary>
        Weather and external damage<span>8 examples</span>
      </summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Example queue: <code>weather_or_external_damage</code>.
        </p>
        <table class="example-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Reported FNOL</th>
              <th>Recorded loss event</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>
                Lightning struck our house chimney directly and damaged the chimney masonry and roof
                tiles.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Direct lightning strike</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                An earthquake shook our home and left fresh cracks across the external walls and
                foundation.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Earthquake</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                A large tree fell onto the garage during the night, crushing the roof and damaging
                the guttering.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Falling trees, poles and other objects</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                Large hailstones struck the house during the afternoon and cracked several roof
                tiles and the conservatory glazing.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Hail</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                The slope behind our house collapsed in a landslide, pushing soil and rocks into the
                garden wall and outbuilding.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Landslide, rockfall</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                Strong winds lifted roof tiles and tore the guttering from our house during a
                windstorm.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Storm</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                A heavy accumulation of snow and ice overloaded the conservatory roof, causing it to
                sag and collapse.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Weight of snow or ice</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                A wild boar broke through the garden fence and damaged the planted beds and exterior
                wall of the shed.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Wild Animals</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="model-example-group">
      <summary>
        Fire and explosion<span>2 examples</span>
      </summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Example queue: <code>fire_or_explosion</code>.
        </p>
        <table class="example-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Reported FNOL</th>
              <th>Recorded loss event</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>
                A fire started in the kitchen and spread to the cabinets, leaving burned surfaces
                and smoke damage throughout the room.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Fire</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                A gas cylinder exploded in the detached garden shed. The blast damaged the shed
                walls and nearby windows.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Explosion</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="model-example-group">
      <summary>
        Glass, electrical and appliance failures<span>3 examples</span>
      </summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Example queue: <code>glass_electrical_or_breakdown</code>.
        </p>
        <table class="example-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Reported FNOL</th>
              <th>Recorded loss event</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>
                While moving a cupboard, I accidentally struck the living-room window with its
                corner and shattered the pane.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Glass Damage</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                An electrical surge from the supply network damaged the television and refrigerator.
                An electrician confirmed the damage followed a voltage spike.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Overvoltage</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                Our washing machine stopped working because its internal motor failed during normal
                use. The engineer found a mechanical breakdown inside the appliance.
              </td>
              <td>
                <strong>Extended warranty</strong>
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          The same damaged item can belong to different event categories. These examples illustrate
          the fixed taxonomy; performance on real insurer notices remains unmeasured.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("quickstart")}>
    <p>
      Use Python 3.12 and the{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/huggingface_hub/guides/cli">HF CLI</a>. Download the
      complete repository:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Terminal"
      code={`hf download nouli-ai/nouli-insurance-loss-event --revision v1.0.1 --local-dir ./nouli-insurance-loss-event
cd nouli-insurance-loss-event
python3.12 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
.venv/bin/python predict.py --checkpoint . --device cpu \\
  --text-file examples/notice.txt
`}
    />
    <p>
      Replace the example file with your own initial notice. The helper applies the release
      preprocessing and returns all 22 probabilities. No hosted API key or separate base-model
      download is required.
    </p>
    <p>
      To run the two-step workflow through{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/nouli-ai/nouli/releases/tag/v0.5.1">Nouli CLI 0.5.1</a>:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Terminal"
      code={`mkdir -p outputs
.venv/bin/python nouli_demo.py --checkpoint . \\
  --nouli "$(command -v nouli)" --device cpu \\
  --text "$(cat examples/notice.txt)" --output outputs/workflow.json
`}
    />
    <p>
      See{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/nouli-ai/nouli-insurance-loss-event/resolve/57e6cf45e097d6280a224ba18cd11f6120eeb28e/GETTING_STARTED.md">
        setup and integration
      </a>{" "}
      for CUDA, offline use, the routing rule and output fields.
    </p>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("provenance")}>
    <p>
      Based on{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/FacebookAI/roberta-large">FacebookAI/roberta-large</a>, with
      data from{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/gratex/GNOTHEIA-synthetic-insurance-dataset-v2">
        Gratex’s GNOTHEIA Synthetic Insurance Dataset v2
      </a>
      . The dataset contains synthetic household and building claim scenarios.{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/nouli-ai/nouli-insurance-loss-event/resolve/57e6cf45e097d6280a224ba18cd11f6120eeb28e/provenance.json">
        Source revisions and provenance
      </a>
      .
    </p>
    <p>
      The repository includes the complete selected classifier, tokenizer and local runtime. The
      workflow and queue mapping are application rules supplied with the example.
    </p>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("limitations")}>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Validated on synthetic English data. Performance on real insurer notices remains unmeasured.
      </li>
      <li>
        Supports a fixed 22-category taxonomy. It always chooses a category for a valid narrative;
        unfamiliar or closely related events can be misclassified, including theft and burglary.
      </li>
      <li>
        Inputs beyond 384 tokens are truncated. Keep the reported cause and circumstances near the
        beginning.
      </li>
      <li>
        The 0.8 review threshold is illustrative; model probabilities do not guarantee correctness.
        Validate thresholds for your workflow.
      </li>
      <li>
        Classifies the reported event; coverage, fraud, pricing and payout decisions are outside its
        scope.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("license-and-citation")}>
    <p>
      Apache-2.0, with the upstream MIT license retained in{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/nouli-ai/nouli-insurance-loss-event/resolve/57e6cf45e097d6280a224ba18cd11f6120eeb28e/BASE_MODEL_LICENSE.txt">
        BASE_MODEL_LICENSE.txt
      </a>
      . See{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/nouli-ai/nouli-insurance-loss-event/resolve/57e6cf45e097d6280a224ba18cd11f6120eeb28e/NOTICE">
        NOTICE
      </a>{" "}
      for attribution.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://nouli.ai">Nouli AI</a>. Nouli Insurance Loss Event. Cite the published
      version and commit.
    </p>
  </ModelSection>
</ModelLayout>
